r.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/release-gate.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-fix.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-claude-login.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/size-trim.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/changelog.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/size-trim.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/docs-rehearsal.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-rule.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/fix-scanner.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-analysis.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/phase1-axylo.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/changelog.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/changelog.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/fix-instructions.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-reaudit.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-analysis.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-claude-login.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/docs-rehearsal.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-reaudit.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/fix-instructions.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/phase1-analysis.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/fix-instructions.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-reaudit.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/release-gate.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-fix.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/release-gate.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/docs-rehearsal.report.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-claude-login.events.jsonl\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/phase1-axylo.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-audit-scope.prompt.md\nreviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-rule.report.md\nacceptance/titles.mjs\nacceptance/check.mjs\nacceptance/run.mjs\nacceptance/README.md\nacceptance/test/titles.test.mjs\nacceptance/provenance.mjs\nacceptance/review.json\nacceptance/matrix.json\nacceptance/report.md\nacceptance/test/check.test.mjs\nacceptance/test/fixtures/stale.json\nacceptance/test/fixtures/results.json\nacceptance/test/fixtures/matrix.json\nacceptance/test/fixtures/chart.browser.test.ts\nacceptance/test/fixtures/missing-title.json\nacceptance/test/fixtures/chart.test.ts\nacceptance/test/fixtures/failed.json\nacceptance/test/fixtures/cases.ts\nacceptance/test/fixtures/non-null-division.test.ts\nacceptance/test/fixtures/docs/02-product-spec.md\napps/docs/pages/stability.md:23:**What it does not cover.** The chart's internal DOM structure and class names, the custom properties listed as internal, the roles the plot uses to talk to assistive technology (chosen without screen-reader rehearsal, and changeable in a minor release if rehearsal shows a better pattern), and the exact pixels of the drawing.\napps/docs/pages/stability.md:39:Tested so far, on Linux: the library's acceptance tests in Chrome and in Playwright's builds of Firefox and WebKit (Playwright's WebKit, not Safari), and the distribution tests in Chrome (they install the packed package, also under a Content Security Policy). Safari itself, Edge, Windows, macOS and phones have not been tested. No screen-reader rehearsal was done for 1.0. What the acceptance checker recorded for this revision is on the [conformance page](conformance.md); see also [Accessibility](accessibility.md#what-has-and-has-not-been-verified).\napps/docs/pages/stability.md:55:## Agent rehearsal\napps/docs/pages/stability.md:57:On release candidate `1e93128`, Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5` each passed three uncoached attempts on each of four fixed line and bar tasks. All 24 attempts passed after the transcript re-audit. In Chrome 158, each saved answer passed the reader checks without error diagnostics on both inputs in the Los Angeles and Tokyo time zones. This agent rehearsal gives no estimate of general success and provides no evidence of human or screen-reader usability or WCAG conformance. The prompt restricted agents to the published docs for guidance, and the audit checked recorded access. The filesystem did not enforce docs-only access.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:97:| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:140:| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's value text: the plot is a native range control whose `aria-valuetext` describes the focused point (decision 0001; earlier drafts said \"accessible name or a polite live region\", which the chosen pattern does not use, and A7 forbids announcements for navigation other than this). On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key. No screen-reader rehearsal is part of 1.0 (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18): what a reader actually speaks on NVDA, VoiceOver or TalkBack has not been checked, and the conformance table says so. The script for such a rehearsal is kept in decision 0001 for when one is done. | R12, R13, WCAG 4.1.2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:149:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:217:Automated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. 1.0 has no evidence for that: no screen reader was run against it, not even by the developer (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18; the rehearsal on five reader and browser pairs that earlier drafts of this section planned was not done). Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says both things in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:226:| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:234:| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:263:| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:286:| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) is used and labelled as such; the developer's screen-reader rehearsal (A3) was dropped from 1.0 by the owner and is stated as not done. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:289:| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:103:| `strict`, `onDiagnostic` | Diagnostics delivery (section 3.5 of the spec). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:132:The registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:319:- The rehearsal script run and recorded on the five pairs, separating what was heard from what was inferred.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:322:Example: \"Exploring a chart with the keyboard\". Docs: Accessibility: what is automatic, what the author supplies, the rehearsal record and its limits.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:359:- The agent rehearsal (DX12), recorded as rehearsal.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:361:### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:380:| Accessibility | axe-core, accessibility-tree snapshots, keyboard scripts, recorded rehearsal | A1–A10. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:397:| One person cannot hand-check real Safari, iOS, Android and Windows screen readers at every milestone. | Automated checks at every milestone; the manual rehearsal at M1, M4 and M8 only, recorded with dates and versions. Device access is a named dependency of those three milestones. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
